{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. Docs-only, minimal, recording the trust assumption hugh ruled at 57df8d25.\n\nWHY THIS ROW IS WORTH MORE THAN ITS SIZE. Three findings on the remote-link lane turned on where the boundary sits, and each cost a review round partly because the boundary was nowhere written down. A reader of internal/app could see the environment being bounded and could not tell whether that was a defence, a determinism property, or habit. Hugh's ruling settles it; this row is what makes the settlement findable by someone reading the code rather than the log.\n\nWHAT I WILL WRITE. That gitseq trusts the ambient Git environment of the operator who runs it; that the operator process environment — routing GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE and GIT_COMMON_DIR, configuration GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM and the GIT_CONFIG_* family, HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME — is outside the threat model; and that defending a hostile operator-controlled environment is a non-goal. I will note that hermeticGitEnvironment's existing GIT_CONFIG stripping is not a defence under this assumption and say what it is instead, rather than leaving a reader to infer that the code disagrees with the page.\n\nWHAT I WILL BE CAREFUL NOT TO DO. Not to write the assumption in a way that implies the environment bound in internal/app is therefore pointless. It is a determinism property — the same repository answers regardless of the invoking shell — and that survives the ruling intact. Two claims about one mechanism, and the page has to distinguish them rather than collapse them, which is the exact failure this lane has spent eight rounds on.\n\nI will find the right page rather than assume it: the conditions offer the architecture security section or an appropriate threat-model page, and I will look at what exists before writing."}